{"name":"Chia","hex":"#eeecb5","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Chia","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A Chinese surname, variant of Jia."]},{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A surname from Hokkien.","A surname from Teochew."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Chia"}],"entries":[{"title":"Amaranthus hypochondriacus","blocks":[{"text":"Amaranthus hypochondriacus is an ornamental plant commonly known as Prince-of-Wales feather or prince's-feather. It is called quelite, bledo and quintonil in Spanish."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/%28MHNT%29_Amaranthus_hypochondriacus_-_Mature_inflorescence_-_Les_Martels%2C_Giroussens_Tarn.jpg/330px-%28MHNT%29_Amaranthus_hypochondriacus_-_Mature_inflorescence_-_Les_Martels%2C_Giroussens_Tarn.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amaranthus_hypochondriacus","wikidata":"Q159611"},{"title":"Mesosphaerum suaveolens","blocks":[{"text":"Mesosphaerum suaveolens, synonym Hyptis suaveolens, chia, pignut, or chan, bukumbar is a branching pseudocereal plant native to tropical regions of Mexico, Central, the West Indies, and South America, as well as being naturalized in tropical parts of Africa, Asia and Australia. It is generally 1–1.5 m (3.3–4.9 ft) tall, occasionally up to 3 m (9.8 ft). Stems are hairy, and square in cross section. Leaves are oppositely arranged, 2–10 cm (0.79–3.94 in) long, with shallowly toothed margins, and emit a strong minty odor if crushed. Flowers are pink or purple, arranged in clusters of 1–5 in the upper leaf axils."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/Hyptis_suaveolens_%28Vilayti_Tulsi%29_in_Hyderabad%2C_AP_W_IMG_0117.jpg/330px-Hyptis_suaveolens_%28Vilayti_Tulsi%29_in_Hyderabad%2C_AP_W_IMG_0117.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mesosphaerum